  "articleSummary": "**Summary:**\n\nThis analytical essay targets engineering and cybersecurity teams to argue that the real threat of autonomous AI agents is not a hypothetical superintelligence, but the compounding multiplication of two existing capabilities: task endurance (autonomous runtime) and orchestrator breadth (concurrent sub-agents). Framing modern LLM threats through the metaphor of *The Terminator*—where the horror lies in relentless, tireless execution rather than genius—the author explains that traditional defensive economic gates fail because attackers use stolen credentials (\"LLMjacking\") to force victims to foot the compute bill. Discussing real-world tools and events like METR benchmarks, Anthropic's Claude Code orchestration, and cloud credential theft, the piece adopts a pragmatic, urgent, and analytical tone to warn that defensive strategies relying on linear human review cannot survive against practically costless, persistent swarms.",
